Recently, a LaBeH<<MATH_INLINE_401>> phase has been synthesized under pressure with a <<MATH_INLINE_402>> rivaling some of the cuprates (110~K at 80~GPa)~<<CMD_502>>. The KB<<MATH_INLINE_403>>H<<MATH_INLINE_404>> phase studied here is predicted to be stable to even lower pressures and exhibit an even higher <<MATH_INLINE_405>>. We therefore wondered if it could be made? Though dynamic stability is easy to assess, it is not the only important criteria for predicting the feasibility of new materials with superlative properties. Thermodynamic stability often necessitates laborious CSP searches that may, or may not, identify the thermodynamic minimum, and cannot pinpoint phases that are metastable and synthesizable. Another type of stability that is easier to probe computationally is kinetic or thermal stability as estimated via molecular dynamics (MD) simulations. Our computations showed that at 12~GPa KB<<MATH_INLINE_406>>H<<MATH_INLINE_407>> decomposes even at 77~K (Figure <<CMD_519>>); further analysis of the structure present at the end of the simulation revealed BH<<MATH_INLINE_408>> molecules, along with species that could be characterized as BH<<MATH_INLINE_409>> units to which H<<MATH_INLINE_410>> was bonded side-on and (H<<MATH_INLINE_411>>B-H-BH<<MATH_INLINE_412>>)<<MATH_INLINE_413>> fragments. Unsurprisingly, this decomposed phase is a PBE-semimetal. The plot of the energy evolution as a function of time of an MD run performed at 77~K illustrates a steep decrease of energy within the first picosecond, indicative of a structural transformation.  At 50~GPa, however, pressure coerced the <<MATH_INLINE_414>> KB<<MATH_INLINE_415>>H<<MATH_INLINE_416>> structure to persist up to at least 165~K, suggesting this phase, should it be made, would not fall apart below its superconducting critical temperature. Notably, while LaBeH<<MATH_INLINE_417>> has a superconducting figure <<CMD_503>> of merit, <<MATH_INLINE_418>>, of 1.236 at 80~GPa, <<MATH_INLINE_419>> for KB<<MATH